[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully. This is a differential geometry question.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA flat compact Riemannian homogeneous manifold - is it necessarily a flat torus?[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the structure. A flat Riemannian manifold is locally [0m
[38;2;124;124;124misometric to Euclidean space. By the Killing-Hopf theorem (Cartan-Hadamard [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem for flat manifolds), a complete flat Riemannian manifold M is of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mform R^n / Γ where Γ is a discrete subgroup of the Euclidean group E(n) = O(n) ⋉[0m[38;2;124;124;124mR^n acting freely and properly discontinuously on R^n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf M is compact, then Γ is a discrete cocompact subgroup of E(n) acting freely -[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthese are called Bieberbach groups.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is: if M is additionally homogeneous (Riemannian homogeneous, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeaning the isometry group acts transitively), must M be a flat torus?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA flat torus is R^n / Λ where Λ is a lattice (a discrete subgroup of R^n, i.e., [0m[38;2;124;124;124mtranslations only). So Γ ⊂ R^n (pure translations).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: does homogeneity force Γ to be a pure translation group?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think. A compact flat manifold R^n/Γ is a torus iff Γ is abelian (iff Γ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconsists only of translations).[0m

